Microsoft Learn: Introduction to Azure Application Gateway

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Introduction to Azure Application Gateway
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Beginner
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn what Azure Application Gateway is, how it works, and when it should be used to deliver web applications securely, efficiently, and at scale.

This module focused on understanding Azure Application Gateway as a Layer 7 load balancing solution, how it routes web traffic, and how it improves application delivery through advanced features such as URL-based routing, SSL termination, and Web Application Firewall (WAF).

Step 1: Understanding Azure Application Gateway

The module introduced Azure Application Gateway as a web traffic load balancer that operates at the Application Layer (Layer 7) of the OSI model.

Unlike Azure Load Balancer, which operates at Layer 4, Application Gateway can make routing decisions based on application-specific information.

Supported protocols:
HTTP
HTTPS
Web applications
Benefits:
Intelligent traffic routing
Application-aware load balancing
SSL termination
Web Application Firewall (WAF)
Enhanced security
Improved scalability

The module emphasized that Application Gateway is specifically designed for web applications.

Step 2: Understanding Layer 7 Load Balancing

A major focus of the module was understanding Layer 7 networking.

Application Gateway operates at:

OSI Layer 7
Application Layer

Because it operates at Layer 7, Application Gateway can inspect:

URLs
Hostnames
HTTP headers
Cookies
Application requests
Example:
www.contoso.com/images
            ↓
Image Server

www.contoso.com/api
            ↓
API Server

The module demonstrated how Application Gateway can route requests differently based on URL paths.

Step 3: Understanding How Azure Application Gateway Works

The training covered the core Application Gateway architecture.

Traffic Flow
Users
   ↓
Application Gateway
   ↓
Backend Pool
   ↓
Applications

Application Gateway sits between users and backend applications.

Core Components:
Component	Purpose
Frontend IP	Receives client requests
Listener	Monitors incoming traffic
Routing Rules	Determines traffic destination
Backend Pool	Contains application servers
Health Probes	Monitors application health

These components work together to provide secure and intelligent application delivery.

Step 4: Understanding Listeners

The module introduced Listeners.

Listeners monitor incoming requests on specific ports and protocols.

Examples:
HTTPS
Port 443
HTTP
Port 80
Functions:
Accept incoming requests
Identify protocols
Forward traffic to routing rules
Key concepts learned:
Every request starts at a listener
Listeners determine how traffic enters the Application Gateway
Step 5: Understanding Backend Pools

The training covered Backend Pools.

Backend pools contain application resources that receive user requests.

Common backend resources:
Azure Virtual Machines
Virtual Machine Scale Sets
Azure App Services
Internal IP addresses
Example:
Backend Pool

WebServer01
WebServer02
WebServer03

Application Gateway distributes traffic across healthy backend resources.

Step 6: Understanding Health Probes

A major focus of the module was health monitoring.

Application Gateway continuously checks backend resources using health probes.

Probe capabilities:
Verify application availability
Detect server failures
Remove unhealthy servers automatically
Example:
Check:
https://server01/health

If the application fails:

Server01 = Unhealthy

Traffic is automatically redirected to healthy servers.

Benefits:
Improved availability
Automatic failover
Reduced downtime

The module emphasized that health probes are critical for maintaining reliable application delivery.

